WEBVTT

00:00:01.50 --> 00:00:06.71
This is journey eighteen of Hewn, the software factory: review agent decisions.

00:00:06.71 --> 00:00:11.73
Hewn has no approval steps, so when a skill would have asked you a question,

00:00:11.73 --> 00:00:17.21
the agent answers it itself and records the answer in the product's decisions file.

00:00:17.21 --> 00:00:21.63
Owners and Viewers want to read those answers afterwards, by stage,

00:00:21.63 --> 00:00:24.74
and see exactly which commit recorded each one.

00:00:24.74 --> 00:00:30.08
This video walks through how it was built: keeping each decision with its commit,

00:00:30.08 --> 00:00:33.52
the decisions API, the Decisions tab, and the tests.

00:00:34.67 --> 00:00:39.79
A user opens a project's Decisions and sees the questions agents answered for

00:00:39.79 --> 00:00:41.46
themselves, newest first.

00:00:41.46 --> 00:00:44.59
Each shows its stage, the question, the answer,

00:00:44.59 --> 00:00:48.31
the rationale and a link to the commit that recorded it,

00:00:48.31 --> 00:00:52.37
and an A D R link when the decision changed the architecture.

00:00:52.37 --> 00:00:54.51
A stage filter narrows the list.

00:00:55.66 --> 00:00:58.03
Here is how a decision is recorded.

00:00:58.03 --> 00:01:01.97
The stage's agent appends the entry to the decisions file,

00:01:01.97 --> 00:01:05.36
and writes an A D R for an architectural decision.

00:01:05.36 --> 00:01:08.76
The worker commits through the write broker first,

00:01:08.76 --> 00:01:11.95
and only then reports the decisions to the API.

00:01:11.95 --> 00:01:17.24
So the API stores each one with the run's latest commit of the decisions file.

00:01:17.59 --> 00:01:19.77
Listing is then a database read.

00:01:19.77 --> 00:01:24.25
The tab asks for the project's decisions, optionally of one stage,

00:01:24.25 --> 00:01:27.24
and gets them newest first with their links.

00:01:27.24 --> 00:01:31.72
When a run reports another decision, the tab reads the list again.

00:01:32.07 --> 00:01:33.48
The classes are few.

00:01:33.48 --> 00:01:38.64
Hewn already kept each reported decision for a stage's own Decisions tab,

00:01:38.64 --> 00:01:43.03
so it gained a commit and an A D R rather than a second table.

00:01:43.03 --> 00:01:45.36
That is recorded as A D R twenty.

00:01:46.51 --> 00:01:51.45
When decisions arrive, the handler finds the run's commits, newest first,

00:01:51.45 --> 00:01:55.45
and takes the first whose paths include the decisions file.

00:01:55.45 --> 00:01:59.92
Every decision is redacted, stored with that commit and its A D R,

00:01:59.92 --> 00:02:02.42
and sent as a DecisionRecorded event.

00:02:02.77 --> 00:02:07.25
The worker reads each entry's A D R link and reports its path,

00:02:07.25 --> 00:02:11.58
which the API checks is a numbered file in the A D R folder.

00:02:11.93 --> 00:02:15.90
Stage ten committed decisions too, but never reported them.

00:02:15.90 --> 00:02:21.42
Now every increment commit that writes the decisions file reports its new entries,

00:02:21.42 --> 00:02:23.30
once each, after the commit.

00:02:24.45 --> 00:02:29.95
The API lists the project's decisions newest first, of one stage when asked.

00:02:29.95 --> 00:02:35.15
Each gets its stage name, a link to its commit on GitHub, and its A D R,

00:02:35.15 --> 00:02:36.38
titled by number.

00:02:37.53 --> 00:02:42.64
Here is an entry in the decisions file, next to how the tab renders it: the date and

00:02:42.64 --> 00:02:47.62
stage, then the question, the answer, the rationale, and the commit that wrote it.

00:02:47.97 --> 00:02:53.34
Choosing a stage puts it in the address, so a link opens the same filtered list,

00:02:53.34 --> 00:02:57.04
and a stage with no decisions offers every stage again.

00:02:58.19 --> 00:03:03.38
The backend tests came first, and failed with not found: there was no route.

00:03:03.38 --> 00:03:08.58
Six now cover the list newest first with stage, rationale, commit and A D R,

00:03:08.58 --> 00:03:14.39
the stage filter, a decision with no commit, a rejected A D R path, an empty project,

00:03:14.39 --> 00:03:15.21
and Viewers.

00:03:15.21 --> 00:03:20.95
The worker tests failed too, until the A D R was reported and stage ten reported its

00:03:20.95 --> 00:03:21.63
decisions.

00:03:21.98 --> 00:03:26.56
End to end, a live test runs a real worker through a whole direction,

00:03:26.56 --> 00:03:31.15
opens Decisions, and follows the stack decision's link to the commit,

00:03:31.15 --> 00:03:34.33
which holds that decision in the decisions file.

00:03:34.68 --> 00:03:38.67
All green, the backend has two hundred and forty four tests,

00:03:38.67 --> 00:03:43.78
the worker one hundred and forty five, and the frontend three new view tests.

00:03:43.78 --> 00:03:49.43
End to end, there are seventeen new tests: five for behaviour, one live pipeline run,

00:03:49.43 --> 00:03:54.01
six visual comparisons with the mocks, and five accessibility checks.

00:03:55.16 --> 00:03:59.01
Here is Kiln Booking, built end to end by a real worker.

00:03:59.01 --> 00:04:02.51
Its decisions run from the brief to the increments.

00:04:02.51 --> 00:04:07.81
Filtering by Stack, then Increments, narrows the list, and Scaffold has none,

00:04:07.81 --> 00:04:10.07
so every stage is one click away.

00:04:10.42 --> 00:04:14.20
Opening the stack decision's commit shows the files it changed,

00:04:14.20 --> 00:04:18.15
and the decisions file at that commit, with the entry highlighted.

00:04:18.50 --> 00:04:22.46
As a Viewer the list reads the same in dark mode, and on a phone.

00:04:22.81 --> 00:04:27.28
A project with no decisions yet says so, and if the list can't load,

00:04:27.28 --> 00:04:29.64
it says why and offers to try again.

00:04:30.79 --> 00:04:32.74
Journey eighteen is complete.

00:04:32.74 --> 00:04:37.79
Every decision an agent records is listed newest first, with its rationale.

00:04:37.79 --> 00:04:41.63
Each links to the commit that wrote it, and to its A D R.

00:04:41.63 --> 00:04:45.67
And the stage filter narrows the list, right in the address.

00:04:45.67 --> 00:04:47.56
Next comes journey nineteen.
